Z||000000||mrgone||04-17-2001||01:32 AM||||is this part interchangeable? can i use one off a different car? the stock replacement fiero thermisor kicks in at rather high temps for me, i like things to be frosty cool, and my high mile engine (240,000k, only 1 of those kilometers was me) prolly wont like the summer. so is there a lower activating thermisor i can pick up at local canadian tire to replace this ?||||reg||2
Z||000001||mrgone||04-17-2001||01:36 AM||||today i bought new pads, calipers, fuel filter, gastank, and a balljoint. was like 400$ so any other cheap repair suggestions out there?||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||04-17-2001||08:58 AM||||I take it you are talking about the thermostat, not a thermistor, which is a electronics componet that creats variable resistance dependent on temperature.<P>Yes, you can replace the thermostat with one that will open at a lower temperature.  Rodney dickman sells one, and it is also a common part that might be able to be found at local autoparts stores.  I dont know the stant part number.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-17-2001||01:08 PM||||If you spend any time in traffic, you'll need a lower temp fan switch also, since while sitting in traffic there's no air flow through the radiator except as provided by the fan, and that will only come on when the switch calls for it- (190 I think, or is it 220?)Anyway, prolly alot warmer than you want. I also met a guy one time who hooked up the radiator fan to the fan switch on his HVAC control, so he could select how fast the fan ran, this bypassed the fan switch entirely. Not that I'd do it, but he said it worked fine.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||bubbajoexx||04-17-2001||07:15 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrgone:<BR><B>today i bought new pads, calipers, fuel filter, gastank, and a balljoint. was like 400$ so any other cheap repair suggestions out there?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>if you want it to run cooler put in a 160 thermostat from canadian tire and ground out the temp senser for the fan and it will come on as soon as you tune on the ignition.<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 04-17-2001).]||||reg||1
